hydrostatic-equilibrium-general-relativi.interpretation:
The equation 
$ \frac{dP}{dr} = -\frac{(\rho + P/c^2)(M + 4\pi r^3 P/c^2)}{r(r-2GM/c^2)} \quad $ 
relates the change in pressure (
iterable{dP/dr}
) with respect to the radial distance (
iterable{r}
) to the mass and pressure within a spherical object. The term 
iterable{\rho}
 represents the energy density, which is analogous to mass density in the relativistic context. The term 
iterable{P/c^2}
 accounts for the contribution of pressure to the effective gravitational source, a key difference from Newtonian physics. The mass 
iterable{M}
 is the total mass enclosed within radius 
iterable{r}
. The term 
iterable{4\pi r^3 P/c^2}
 represents the contribution of the internal pressure to the effective mass-energy enclosed. The denominator 
iterable{r(r-2GM/c^2)}
 involves the Schwarzschild radius (
iterable{2GM/c^2}
), indicating how spacetime curvature affects the pressure gradient. A positive 
iterable{dP/dr}
 would mean pressure increases with radius, which is unstable, while a negative 
iterable{dP/dr}
 indicates pressure decreases outward, leading to equilibrium.

hydrostatic-equilibrium-general-relativi.limits_and_boundary:
In the limit of weak gravity and low pressure (i.e., when 
iterable{GM/c^2 $o 0}
 and 
iterable{P/c^2 $o 0}
), the equation reduces to the Newtonian hydrostatic equilibrium equation: 
iterable{dP/dr = -G M(r) \rho(r) / r^2}
. As 
iterable{r}
 approaches the Schwarzschild radius (
iterable{2GM/c^2}
), the denominator approaches zero, leading to a singularity in the pressure gradient, which is expected at the event horizon of a black hole or for extremely compact objects where gravity is strong.

